# SELF-GATING. Worth stating precisely, because it is easy to overclaim: this
# file was NEVER self-gated in this repo. The retired rules covered other paths
# and no `**/CODEOWNERS` or `*` rule existed here, so no self-gate is being
# removed. What guards an edit to this register is the required approving
# review above, plus HIGH_RISK escalation of CODEOWNERS itself — so a change
# that quietly deletes a path from it, or weakens the HIGH_RISK regex, is read
# by the strongest reviewer under a required review.
#
# WHY THIS FILE STILL EXISTS. Every path below was marked high-risk for a real
# reason, and that reason is worth keeping even though the founder is no longer
# the reviewer. This file is now a REGISTER OF SENSITIVE PATHS: prose for a
# human or agent reviewer, not a routing rule. The signal also stays
# load-bearing in machine terms — see ESCALATION below.
#
# CATEGORIES THAT DELIBERATELY DO NOT APPLY HERE. enscrive-docs has no high-risk
# service paths; under ADR-0003 / PRD §6.7 the only applicable rule was
# dependency manifests. The workflows entry covers the shared supply-chain
# surface every repo carries.
#
# ESCALATION — measured, not asserted.
#
#   .github/scripts/pr-review.sh escalates the reviewer model Sonnet -> Opus
#   (HIGH_RISK) for ALL 2 of the paths registered below. Verified path-by-path
#   on 2026-08-15 by matching each registered pattern against the live regex,
#   not by reading the regex and assuming.
#
#   That coverage is NOT inherited. Before this change it was partial, and two
#   distinct defects were found while measuring it:
#     * paths this register named simply were not in the regex; and
#     * alternatives written with a leading slash — /proto/, /migrations/,
#       /audit, /auth, /esm/ — never matched a REPOSITORY-ROOT path at all,
#       because grep saw no preceding separator. Escalation for those was
#       silently dead. They are now anchored (^|["/]).
#
#   The FIRST attempt at that fix used (^|/) and was ALSO wrong, which is
#   worth recording because the failure was in the verification, not the
#   patch: $FILES is a COMPACT SINGLE-LINE JSON array
#   ([".github/CODEOWNERS","..."]), so `^` matches only before the opening
#   bracket and a root path is QUOTE-preceded. It was "verified" against bare
#   path strings rather than that JSON, so the check reproduced the assumption
#   instead of testing it. The assertions in
#   .github/scripts/pr-review-verdict-test.sh now run the live regex against
#   the exact shape the script greps (ENS-4350).
#
#   If you add a path to this register, add it to that regex and re-measure.
#   A register that claims protection it does not have is worse than no
#   register: it is the failure mode this whole change set exists to remove.
#
# CAN THE REVIEWER APPROVE ITS OWN HARNESS? Yes, and that is a genuine
# narrowing of control — stated here rather than left to be discovered. With
# owner routing gone, a PR editing .github/scripts/pr-review.sh or
# .github/workflows/ can carry the bot's own approval. What still stands
# between that and main:
#   * the bot CANNOT MERGE. AUTO_MERGE=false, and allow_auto_merge was set
#     false across the fleet (A-034 / ENS-3513) after the reviewer squash-merged
#     enscrive-embed #92 on its own sub-threshold approval. Approval is not
#     merge; the orchestrator is the arbiter and is a different actor.
#   * those paths escalate to Opus (HIGH_RISK), so the approval given is at
#     least the strongest available.
# If that is judged insufficient, the fix is branch protection requiring a
# NON-BOT approval on .github/** — not a paragraph in this file.
